{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"copy-semantics","title":"Copy Semantics: Shallow vs Deep","group":"Copy, Move & Smart Pointers","order":32,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Copying an object calls its copy constructor (new object) or copy assignment operator (existing object). If the class owns a raw resource, the default member-wise copy is <em>shallow</em> — and wrong.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Copy constructor vs copy assignment</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Tag {\n    int v = 0;\n    Tag() = default;\n    Tag(const Tag&amp; o) : v(o.v) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"copy ctor\\n\"; }\n    Tag&amp; operator=(const Tag&amp; o) { v = o.v; std::cout &lt;&lt; \"copy assign\\n\"; return *this; }\n};\nvoid byValue(Tag) {}\nint main() {\n    Tag a;\n    Tag b = a;       // copy ctor: b is being created (the '=' here is not assignment)\n    Tag c(a);        // copy ctor\n    c = b;           // copy assignment: c already exists\n    byValue(a);      // copy ctor for the parameter\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>copy ctor\ncopy ctor\ncopy assign\ncopy ctor</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bug\"></i>The shallow-copy bug</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — double free (ASan)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\nclass Buffer {\n    char* data_;\npublic:\n    explicit Buffer(const char* s) : data_(new char[std::strlen(s) + 1]) { std::strcpy(data_, s); }\n    ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; }\n    // no copy constructor → the compiler copies the *pointer*\n};\nint main() {\n    Buffer a(\"hello\");\n    Buffer b = a;        // b.data_ == a.data_\n}                        // both destructors delete[] the same block → double free</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy-check\"></i>Deep copy done right</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nclass Buffer {\n    std::size_t size_;\n    char* data_;\npublic:\n    explicit Buffer(const char* s) : size_(std::strlen(s)), data_(new char[size_ + 1]) {\n        std::copy(s, s + size_ + 1, data_);\n    }\n    ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; }\n\n    Buffer(const Buffer&amp; o) : size_(o.size_), data_(new char[o.size_ + 1]) {   // deep copy\n        std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + size_ + 1, data_);\n    }\n    Buffer&amp; operator=(Buffer o) {          // copy-and-swap: `o` is already a copy\n        swap(*this, o);                    // take its contents; old ones die with `o`\n        return *this;\n    }\n    friend void swap(Buffer&amp; a, Buffer&amp; b) noexcept {\n        std::swap(a.size_, b.size_);\n        std::swap(a.data_, b.data_);\n    }\n    void set(std::size_t i, char c) { data_[i] = c; }\n    const char* str() const { return data_; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Buffer a(\"hello\");\n    Buffer b = a;          // independent copy\n    b.set(0, 'j');\n    Buffer c(\"x\");\n    c = a;                 // assignment\n    Buffer&amp; alias = c;\n    c = alias;             // self-assignment is safe\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.str() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.str() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c.str() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hello jello hello</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Copy-and-swap</div><p>Copy-and-swap gives the <strong>strong exception guarantee</strong> (if the copy throws, <code>*this</code> is untouched) and handles self-assignment for free.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Hold resources in members that already know how to copy themselves (<code>std::string</code>, <code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::unique_ptr</code>) — then you write <em>no</em> copy code at all (Rule of Zero).</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Owning raw pointers (<code>char*</code>, <code>T*</code> from <code>new</code>) in a class without writing — or deleting — the copy operations.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between a shallow and a deep copy?</summary><div><p>A shallow copy duplicates the member values, so two objects' pointers end up pointing at the same resource (leading to double frees and aliasing bugs). A deep copy allocates a fresh resource and copies the pointed-to contents, giving each object its own.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When is the copy constructor called?</summary><div><p>When a new object is initialised from an existing one: <code>T b = a;</code>, <code>T b(a);</code>, passing by value, returning by value (unless elided), throwing/catching by value, and when containers copy elements.</p></div></details></section>"}